; 46-calc.kurt

; `calc` is a toggle (`calc on` / `calc off`) that switches on automatic
; arithmetic simplification of `+` and `*` inside expressions, before they
; are checked -- so `1 + 1` is simplified to `2` before Kurt even looks for a
; proof.

load equality
infix "+" 30 30

calc on
1 + 1 = 2             ; simplified to `2 = 2`, then "equal-intro"

; With `calc off` (the default), the same claim really does fail: nothing
; simplifies `1 + 1` anymore, and no axiom relates it to `2` (see 16-expect):
calc off
expect "ProofError"
    1 + 1 = 2

; With `calc on`, comparing two numbers is also a calculation:
infix < 20 20
bool  < 0
calc on
2 + 2 < 5             ; `4 < 5`, true "by calc"
expect "ProofError"
    2 + 2 = 5         ; `4 = 5` is false, so it can't be proven
